    {
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "// Add Waiting Label runs first (inline, before Split Attachments) so the label\n// is guaranteed to land before any processing begins -- a parallel fan-out from\n// Gmail Trigger was tried instead and empirically never executed the dead-end\n// branch at all (confirmed via a direct Gmail API check showing the label was\n// never applied), so this reverts to a plain sequential chain.\n//\n// Add Waiting Label's own output is the Gmail \"labels: add\" API response --\n// it carries the same message id/subject/from as the original trigger item,\n// but NOT the original binary attachment (Gmail's messages.modify response\n// never includes attachment bytes). Pull the binary from the original Gmail\n// Trigger node's output instead, matched by message id, while everything else\n// (filename, subject, from, messageId) comes from the current input item.\n//\n// One Gmail message can carry multiple attachments (attachment_0, attachment_1,\n// ... on the SAME trigger item). ai-service processes one file per call, so\n// split each attachment into its own item with a normalized 'data' binary key --\n// matching the shape the webhook path already produces -- so the rest of the\n// pipeline (Call AI Service, Insert Form DB, ...) doesn't need to know which\n// channel a form arrived through. Pure in-memory reshaping, no external calls,\n// so this is safe as a Code node despite the project's usual Code-node caution\n// (that caution is specifically about the slow, CPU-bound ai-service call\n// itself -- see RUNNING.md).\nconst newItems = [];\nconst triggerItems = $('Gmail Trigger').all();\nfor (const item of $input.all()) {\n  const triggerItem = triggerItems.find(t => t.json.id === item.json.id) || triggerItems[0];\n  const binaryKeys = Object.keys((triggerItem && triggerItem.binary) || {});\n  for (const key of binaryKeys) {\n    const bin = triggerItem.binary[key];\n    newItems.push({\n      json: {\n        filename: bin.fileName || `${key}.bin`,\n        subject: item.json.subject || item.json.Subject || null,\n        from: item.json.from || item.json.From || null,\n        messageId: item.json.id || null,\n      },\n      binary: {\n        data: bin,\n      },\n    });\n  }\n}\nreturn newItems;"
      },
